# Secure Refresh Token Cookie Migration Plan

Migrate the Refresh Token storage from local storage (`localStorage`) to an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and `SameSite` cookie to mitigate XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerabilities.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The backend CORS configuration in `Program.cs` is already configured with `.AllowCredentials()` and `.WithOrigins("http://localhost:4200")`, which allows credentials/cookies to be securely passed between the client and server. No modifications to `Program.cs` CORS settings are required.

> [!WARNING]
> Since the Refresh Token is stored in a cookie scoped to the backend domain, client-side Javascript cannot access it directly. The refresh token parameter inside `UserResponse` returned by the `Login`, `Register`, and `RefreshToken` JSON payloads will be set to an empty string (`""`) to prevent exposing it.

## Open Questions

None. The proposed cookie settings (`HttpOnly = true`, `Secure = true`, `SameSite = SameSiteMode.Lax`, `Path = "/"`) are standard best practices for localhost dev and secure web applications.

